LATEST IMPLEMENTATION — MB-03B
AppendStoreV2 is a separate, explicit profile 2 API. initialize(path, &ChainV2)
accepts only an absent or empty final directory under an existing parent, using a
complete already accepted chain. It does not implicitly adopt or migrate another
store, create a genesis, grant funds or trust a peer's configuration. StoreV2 and
immutable MSS2 profile 1 remain unchanged. Exact MNM2 and MRB2 bytes and all prior
fixtures and hash domains are preserved.

The clean root layout contains manifest.v2, blocks/ and selection.v2. Block filenames
use exactly 20 decimal height digits followed by .mrb2. The append selector is MSU2,
exactly 280 bytes: MSU2 magic, u16 version 2, u16 profile 2, u64 generation, an unchanged
232-byte MSS2 snapshot, and a 32-byte SHA256d checksum over the domain
MIRROR_STORAGE_APPEND_SELECTION_V2 followed by the first 248 bytes. Nested MSS2 keeps
profile 1 and generation zero. The outer generation starts at zero at any initial
accepted height, then every published child advances both generation and height by
one. Generation must not exceed height. A call may report uncertain publication
after the new generation has already been selected. Overflow is checked defensively,
but the valid finite profile cannot reach u64 overflow. Generation is neither
consensus weight nor authority for global freshness or rollback protection.

open_writer and inspect require independently supplied ManifestIdentityV2 and
AppendCheckpointV2 { generation, chain: ChainReplayExpectationV2 }. Every network
identity, generation, height, tip and ledger-root pin must match. Never derive
expected pins from the untrusted record itself. Whole-chain replay validates every
block and reconstructs accounts, membership, permanent records, population, approvals
and resource counters together. A saved cache or counter cannot become runtime
authority.

append(&mut self, BlockV2) validates a clone through ChainV2 before disk I/O. An
invalid candidate leaves original state and bytes unchanged and the handle usable.
Before writing, it fences the handle, verifies the root inode, fully replays the
selected disk history against retained pins, and compares that complete chain with
memory. A disk discrepancy leaves the handle fenced. It creates, writes and
synchronizes the new block, synchronizes blocks/, creates, writes and synchronizes
selection.pending, compares the old selector again, renames the pending file over
selection.v2, and synchronizes the root. Only after success does it install the
chain and selector together in memory, clear the fence, and return the new checkpoint
for the caller to retain.

UpdateIncomplete means publication was not attempted, although partial artifacts
may remain. PublicationUncertain means publication may have happened. Both carry the
locally validated candidate checkpoint, which is not proof that publication succeeded.
Any write error blocks further appends. chain(), selection() and checkpoint() return
Fenced. root(), access(), is_fenced() and last_confirmed_checkpoint() remain available.
The last-confirmed value describes previously successful memory; after an uncertain
result, it does not establish which state is selected on disk.

reconcile(self, expected) consumes a writer and retains the same lock throughout
full selected replay and file/directory synchronization. It returns an unfenced
writer only if every step succeeds; failure returns no handle. open_writer likewise
resynchronizes the validated manifest and blocks, the blocks directory, the selector
and the root. It compares the selector before and after synchronization and verifies
stable root identity. Inspection holds a shared lock and performs no application
write, synchronization or repair. Revalidation after a failed synchronization
establishes the newly visible, checked and synchronized state; it does not prove
that an earlier synchronization succeeded.

CRITICAL CURRENT LIMIT: normal opening, inspection and reconciliation require a
strict clean layout. They preserve and reject extra next-height blocks, pending
selectors, partial files and unknown data. No pending marker is promoted, no artifact
is deleted, and no largest generation is chosen automatically. A complete old or
new selected state can be reopened under independent pins, but residual preparation
may cause explicit rejection and leave the store unavailable. This availability gap
is the next task, MB-03C; automatic repair is not implemented.

OWNERSHIP, LIMITS AND TRUST
Linux cooperative locks attach to the root directory inode. A writer holds an
exclusive lock; inspectors hold shared locks for their handle lifetimes. There is
no clone, raw descriptor, mutable-chain accessor, lock upgrade, PID authority or
stale-lock cleanup. The root must remain stable. Kernel ownership ends when the last
descriptor sharing the open file description closes, including temporarily inherited
fork descriptors. Preserve isolation of parent process tests; do not hide ownership
failures with sleeps, retries or explicit unlocks.

Trusted local owners and parent directories, correct locking, atomic rename, and
correct file/directory synchronization are assumptions. Static symlink, type, hardlink
and inode checks are not a sandbox against a hostile same-user process. Local Linux
tmpfs/overlayfs checks and Ubuntu CI apply only as recorded. WSL2's native Linux
filesystem is intended, but WSL, DrvFS, native Windows, NFS and SMB remain unverified.

Chain budgets remain 4,096 blocks including genesis, 16 MiB of complete MRB2 data,
and 1,048,576 cumulative abstract state/configuration items. Do not evict history,
prune retained records or increase caps silently. MNM2 is at most 2,781,636 bytes;
MSU2 is exactly 280 bytes. The selected encoded-payload maximum is 19,559,132 bytes,
not a total resident-memory limit. Candidate, history and buffer overhead remain
separately bounded. Reopening preflights every file, count and aggregate size before
reading blocks, using bounded length-plus-one sentinel reads. Root and block-directory
enumeration are bounded. An append temporarily retains the old and pending 280-byte
selectors; unexplained tails are rejected. Future residual recovery must specify its
combined read budget separately, rather than applying selected-only limits to
unbounded leftover data.

